WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.760
<v Anna>Imagine you promised your app would be available 99.9% of

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.600
<v Anna>the time. That still allows roughly nine hours of downtime

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.400
<v Anna>a year.

00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:12.982
<v Greg>Site Reliability Engineering, or SRE, turns those promises into math

00:00:13.017 --> 00:00:16.465
<v Greg>we can track. The key tools are service level objectives,

00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:19.019
<v Greg>or SLOs, and the error budgets tied to them.

00:00:19.437 --> 00:00:22.797
<v Anna>By defining how reliable a service must be, we can

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:25.837
<v Anna>decide when it's safe to launch new features and when

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:28.557
<v Anna>it's time to pause and fix instability.

00:00:29.377 --> 00:00:32.657
<v Anna>An error budget is the small slice of allowable failure

00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:38.177
<v Anna>built into an SLO. If our target is 99.9% uptime,

00:00:38.257 --> 00:00:41.457
<v Anna>that gives us about forty minutes of downtime each month.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.585
<v Greg>As outages or performance issues occur, we "spend" that budget.

00:00:46.189 --> 00:00:48.998
<v Greg>When it's gone, engineering focuses on reliability work instead of

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:50.252
<v Greg>shipping new features.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.230
<v Anna>This approach keeps everyone aligned.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.470
<v Anna>Product managers see how instability eats into development time, while

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.910
<v Anna>engineers know exactly when to slow their release pace.

00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:04.507
<v Anna>Error budgets aren't just for ops teams.

00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:08.187
<v Anna>They spark conversations about risk across the organisation.

00:01:08.513 --> 00:01:11.172
<v Greg>When the budget burns down faster than expected, it's a

00:01:11.218 --> 00:01:13.923
<v Greg>sign our releases might be too risky or our SLO

00:01:13.981 --> 00:01:15.015
<v Greg>is unrealistic.

00:01:15.424 --> 00:01:19.264
<v Anna>Teams can agree to slow deployments, add more testing, or

00:01:19.344 --> 00:01:22.624
<v Anna>even adjust the SLO, if customer impact warrants it.

00:01:23.184 --> 00:01:26.304
<v Anna>The data helps remove emotion from those decisions.
